WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:00.880
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Can you

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.310
get a sense of the breadth
of Google Cloud Platform

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.100
by looking at Compute products
in just a few minutes?

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:06.730
Let's find out.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.013
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.430
Arguably, the Hello
World in cloud

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:19.270
is to provision, start, and
connect a virtual machine.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.510
Google Compute Engine enables
you to create single VM

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:26.590
instances from scratch by
specifying a region, a machine

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.340
type, an OS image, and
other optional parameters.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.980
Note how a cost
estimate is provided

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.630
while you configure the VM.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.270
A few options that I'd
really like to point out is

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.360
the ability to add
GPUs or TPUs-- that is,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.310
Tensor Processing Units--

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.820
to your instance, and
the plethora of OS images

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.690
that are supported, including
numerous Linux distros and MS

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.760
Windows, plus the support to use
a custom image if you see fit.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.460
Once you click Create, give
the system a little time

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.050
to boot your instance,
and then you'll

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.140
be able to SSH into it
right from a browser.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.209
You could also have
created a new VM

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:06.460
from a previously saved
template or from one

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.580
of the numerous, ready-to-go
solutions on the Marketplace.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.120
Now the marketing group would be
upset if I didn't take a moment

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.870
and list all the other advanced
features of GC, including

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.890
finegrained security
access control,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.570
HTTPS connectivity, truly
stunning live migration

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.170
of running applications,
preemptible VMs, and more.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.400
These machines are on
the Google network,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.580
which means that they'll
have minimal latency

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.250
and great throughput for
everything that requires

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.570
accessing remote services.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.830
Now, one of the reasons that
Compute Engine is so nice

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.560
is that it gives you
complete control.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.450
But with that control
comes a great deal

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.520
of management overhead.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.930
Thankfully, Google
Cloud Platform

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.980
has offerings in the other
side of the spectrum, where

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.520
you just focus on code
and we handle the rest.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.790
Of course, we're talking
about Google Cloud Functions.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.340
The serverless flavor
of a Hello World

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.650
has to be built on
Cloud Functions.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.170
Here, we provide a
simple snippet of code

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:08.500
to listen to image file
uploads into a storage bucket

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.650
to create thumbnails for
each one of them on the fly.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.270
To trigger this
code to run, simply

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.400
upload an image to your
bucket and the function

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.420
will do the rest.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.220
Now, Cloud Functions
support a lot

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.580
of triggers, such as changes
in a database, messages

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.520
from pub/sub, or even
Compute Engine instances

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.410
changing state.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.560
Cloud Functions can also be
invoked using a standard HTTP

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.590
request, meaning a
user visits a URL

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.820
and the function does the work.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.340
These functions can be
deployed in any region

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.200
from a single
project, and they're

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.970
authenticated with the
rest of GCP services,

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.450
making integration with
other products and APIs

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.460
from the platform
quite natural and easy.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.710
Now here's the best part.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.600
For Cloud Functions
to work, you don't

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.750
have to provision a cluster
or deal with server configs.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.100
We do that for you.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.380
And since most of the work
you do with Cloud Functions

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.660
is temporal, it means
you only pay for the code

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.700
that you're running
while it's running.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.160
No traffic, no cost.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.980
Now, one challenge we
have with Cloud Functions

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.760
is that while
they're easy to use,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.210
they make large-scale app
development a little trickier.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.570
So if you need something
larger than Cloud Functions,

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.280
but still want
serverless benefits,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.980
you should check out
App Engine, which

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.770
has the same scale-on-demand
but a lot more

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.280
configurations for developers,
such as services, versioning,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.750
and traffic splitting.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.290
And of course, you can't
talk about cloud in 2019

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.270
without talking
about containers.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.250
The Hello World
in container-land

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.700
is to deploy a Docker
image to an orchestrator.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.680
Since Google open
sourced Kubernetes,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.380
we've gotten a lot of
feedback about what works

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.060
and the challenges involved
with deploying containers.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.050
This led to the development
of Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.920
or GKE, a fully-managed
version of Kubernetes.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.930
You can deploy your
containerized applications

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.290
to GKE which offers a production
infrastructure guaranteeing

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.080
uptime, while providing
rich dashboard metrics

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.580
and automated operations from
auto-scaling to node repairs,

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.600
to Kubernetes version upgrades.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.340
To deploy a
containerized workload,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.430
simply describe the compute,
memory, and storage resources

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.490
your application containers
require, and Kubernetes Engine

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.579
will provision and manage the
underlying cloud resources

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.820
automatically.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:23.160
With GKE, you can also
attach persistent storage.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.360
You can even run stateful
workloads such as databases.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.450
You can also make
use of GPUs and TPUs

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.060
But best of all, your
Kubernetes workloads

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.330
are portable across all
Kubernetes implementations,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.000
from your development
environment to GKE,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.760
to other cloud or
on-premises installations.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.400
In summary, GCP supports all
types of compute workloads.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.510
It will run existing Linux and
Windows applications on Compute

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.990
Engine, containerized
workloads on Kubernetes Engine,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.190
and functions or apps on Cloud
Functions and App Engine.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.140
Give us the artifact
of your choice

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.570
and we'll provide a fast
and reliable underlying

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.290
infrastructure.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.230
Consider taking free code labs
linked in the description below

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.150
to explore these
products, and look forward

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:10.660
to upcoming videos on data,
storage, machine learning,

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.520
and more overviews.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.490
If you like this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:16.702
and share.

00:05:16.702 --> 00:05:20.300
See you soon for another
"GCP Essentials" video.

